ROCclr (gfx7/gfx8, ROCm 10.0): on CIK the CP ignores the AQL acquire and
release fence-scope bits, and nothing else maintains the compute shader's TC
across a command boundary. One PM4 ACQUIRE_MEM packet
(TC_ACTION_ENA|TC_WB_ACTION_ENA, whole address range), published in its own AQL
ring slot, closes the three directions in which that silently corrupts data:

  1. Ahead of every kernel dispatch, so a kernel cannot be served stale shader-TC
     lines (the reader direction).
  2. Ahead of the barrier that a copy-engine transfer waits on, so a copy engine
     cannot read memory whose dirty lines are still in the caches (the
     external-reader direction, same queue).
  3. Ahead of an event-record barrier, so a copy engine on another queue cannot
     read memory whose dirty lines are still in the caches (the
     external-reader direction, foreign queue).

All three sites are gated on `dev().isa().versionMajor() <= 8` and on the new
`CLR_GFX8_TC_INVALIDATE` flag, which is on by default, so one binary can be
measured both ways. Site 3 has its own flag, `CLR_GFX8_TC_RECORD_FENCE`.

REASON 1: THE READER DIRECTION
------------------------------
The repro is torch on gfx803: `y = torch.bmm(a,b); z.copy_(y)` in a loop over a
caching allocator that recycles about five virtual addresses. The first read of
the GEMM output returns the bytes that lived at that address before the GEMM ran,
in scattered aligned chunks. A second read of the same tensor is correct. A
GPU-to-GPU copy at the wrong moment freezes the stale bytes into the destination
permanently. What the fault is, and what it is not (all measured on the card,
with the mechanisms themselves instrumented):

  * The new data is provably in VRAM while the reader is wrong. Walking the
    process's own 4-level page tables from the driver and reading the resulting
    physical page directly (debug-kernel `gfx803_ptwalk`/`gfx803_readphys`) shows
    the correct GEMM result about 30 ms after the copy that read something else.
  * It is not a write-completion race. 20 ms and 500 ms of idle before the read
    change nothing. `AMD_SERIALIZE_KERNEL=3` changes nothing.
  * It is not the GPUVM TLB. An explicit all-VMID `VM_INVALIDATE_REQUEST`
    (debugfs knob, ack-polled) leaves the bug at its baseline rate: 8/60 against
    a 7/60 control.
  * It is not fence scope. `AMD_OPT_FLUSH=0` (SYSTEM-scope acquire and release on
    every dispatch) does not help. That is the direct evidence that this ISA's CP
    does not act on the scope bits at all.
  * It is a read cache. Reading an unrelated 1 MB or 64 MB buffer between the
    writer and the reader makes the fault vanish (0/30, twice). 256 KB is not
    enough. Reading the tensor itself before the copy makes it worse (27/30
    against 16/30), because the read re-populates the stale lines.
  * A plain extra kernel launch plus sync between writer and reader makes it
    worse, not better (20-24/40). So more traffic is not the cure.

What does fix it, twice at 0/40 with interleaved controls reproducing at 5/40 and
3/40, is `hipModuleLoadData()`. The only cache-related side effect of that call is
ROCr's `GpuAgent::InvalidateCodeCaches()` (amd_gpu_agent.cpp), which for
`GetMajorVersion() < 10` emits ACQUIRE_MEM with
TC_ACTION_ENA|TC_WB_ACTION_ENA: "invalidate the shader TC and write it back".
Nothing else in the ROCm data path ever emits it. Dispatch-to-dispatch coherence
is expressed only through the AQL SCACQUIRE and SCRELEASE scope bits, and
`rocvirtual.cpp`'s own comment claims an AGENT acquire "invalidates I, K and L1".
This card's CP and microcode (0x2da) do not act on that for the TC. gfx9 and
newer get an explicit GL1/GL2 invalidate from the CP, which is why the same stack
is clean there.

Because ROCm relies on the scope bits alone, the corruption is invisible to a
workload that never re-reads a VA that a kernel already cached. That is why raw
`hipMalloc`/`hipFree` C++ replicas of the identical kernel sequence stay clean
while torch, whose allocator hands the same handful of VAs back every iteration,
hits it constantly. It is a ROCm defect and not a torch defect. Any consumer that
recycles addresses is exposed: MIGraphX, ONNX Runtime, vLLM, or plain HIP with a
pool allocator.

REASON 2: THE EXTERNAL-READER DIRECTION
---------------------------------------
Symptom, after (1) landed: `tools/correctness-suite/torch_op_suite.py` still
failed 3 to 5 `conv2d [torch.float32]` cases (fan-in 1152, 2880, and 5760) with
1.2 to 2.5 absolute error. It is not a convolution accuracy problem. The
convolution is correct, and only the host copy of it is wrong.
`tools/tc-staleness/conbrace3.py` reduces the suite to one conv per shape with an
immediate host read against a CPU fp64 reference, and classifies each anomaly by
site: device-side inputs, error recomputed on the GPU, and error after the host
copy. Out of 240 checks it gives 90 anomalies, all of one kind:

    STATS {'anom': 90, 'bad_input': 0, 'bad_gpu': 0, 'copy_only': 90}

So the inputs on the device are intact, and the convolution output in GPU memory
is correct to 1e-4 when checked on the GPU, while the host copy of that same
buffer is wrong. The differing elements sit in scattered blocks of 64 floats,
which is exactly one cache line. A kernel that reads the buffer gets the right
answer, because the slot from (1) precedes it. SDMA reading it gets the pre-write
answer, because a copy engine reads DRAM and never looks at the shader TC.
Corroborating measurements, on the same shapes in the same process:

  * It does not depend on this patch's first site: 120 of 320 anomalies with (1)'s
    knob on, and the same 120 with it off.
  * It is deterministic and not a race: the anomaly count and pattern repeat.
  * `GPU_FORCE_BLIT_COPY_SIZE=100000` gives 0/240. That flag sets
    `dev().settings().sdmaCopyThreshold_`, and the deciding branch is
    `useShaderCopyPath` in `rocblit.cpp`: above the threshold a pageable D2H goes
    through `DmaBlitManager::readBuffer` and SDMA, below it through a kernel copy,
    which is a dispatch and so gets (1)'s slot.
  * `GPU_BLIT_ENGINE_TYPE=3` does nothing, because that flag does not select
    between those two paths for a pageable host destination.

The fix is the same slot, submitted where the runtime already commits to making a
ring's results readable by someone else: `VirtualGPU::releaseGpuMemoryFence()`,
which is what `DmaBlitManager::hsaCopyStagedOrPinned()` calls immediately before it
hands a transfer to SDMA. It goes after that function's own barrier, and a second
barrier follows it. Both halves of that order are load-bearing, and the code
comment at the call site states why. Measured with one binary and the knob
toggled: 0/240 on, 90/240 off, and the op suite goes from 197-199/202 to 202/202.

REASON 3: THE CROSS-QUEUE HANDOFF
---------------------------------
Symptom after (1) and (2) were in: `tools/tc-staleness/multistream2.py`, a producer
conv on a `torch.cuda.Stream` side stream, an event record, `wait_event` on the
default stream, then a host copy of the side stream's result, still returns stale
bytes on roughly 1 copy in 50. `tools/tc-staleness/ms3.py` (checked in with this
change) is the same shape with each anomaly classified, and it says where the loss
is: the first host copy is wrong, a *kernel* clone taken immediately afterwards is
right, and a second host copy after a sync is right too. So nothing is destroyed;
the copy engine read DRAM before the producer's dirty lines reached it.

Two obvious fixes measured as no help at all, and one that measured as worse than
nothing, all on one binary with knobs:

  * Attaching the producer's completion signal to the barrier in front of the (2)
    writeback, on the consumer's ring, in one place or in both: 11/960 with the
    attach, 11/960 without. The barrier's `dep_signal` is honoured by the CIK
    queue's AQL packet processor, and the CP does not consult that when it pulls a
    raw PM4 packet out of the same ring.
  * A CP-side `PM4 WAIT_REG_MEM` (the packet the kernel driver itself uses for
    pipeline sync on this IP, `sid.h` 0x3C, 7 dwords, MEM_SPACE bit 4, poll
    interval in its own dword, encoding taken from
    `gfx_v8_0_ring_emit_pipeline_sync`) in front of the writeback: it does not
    execute in an AQL ring. It stalls the queue for every comparison value tested,
    including the one that means "do not wait", and the stall is real (the debug
    kernel logged `Resetting wave fronts (cpsch)`). ROCr only ever emits this
    packet on its legacy PM4 ring. Reverted.
  * Routing the cross-stream copy through the shader instead of the copy engine:
    13/960, i.e. no better than the control, because the blit that was redirected
    is not the transfer that was exposed. Reverted. It also would have cost the
    copy bandwidth of every such transfer for nothing.

What does close it is the producer-side flush, which is the place the caches can
actually be pushed from: `VirtualGPU::submitMarker()`, on the queue that is about
to be waited on, when the marker is an event record (`vcmd.coalesceEvent() != 0`),
which is the same test the marker's own coalescing logic uses.

This is a different thing from what the earlier version of this header said was
impossible at that spot. What is impossible there is an extra *barrier*, because a
barrier takes a tracked completion signal and the marker path's own signal
bookkeeping (`ShouldCoalesceMarker`, `last_barrier_hw_event_`) then ends up holding
a recycled `ProfilingSignal`: the process dies of `malloc(): smallbin double linked
list corrupted` within a few hundred markers, both through
`releaseGpuMemoryFence(kSkipCpuWait)` and through a hand-built
barrier/slot/barrier. The raw slot taken alone publishes no signal and touches no
tracker, which is exactly the condition the earlier text told the next person to
satisfy, and it is safe here for the same reason it is safe at the other two sites.

Measured on the card with one binary and `CLR_GFX8_TC_RECORD_FENCE` toggled:
`ms3.py` 0/2000 with the site on, 21/960 with it off; `multistream2.py` 0/1600;
`ms4.py` (same shape, but the consumer copies into pinned host memory, and a second
arm where two producer streams fan into one consumer) 0/2400 on, with its control
reproducing; `xprobe2.py` (cross-queue kernel reader) 0/40; `pinnedhost.py` 0/60;
`conbrace3.py` (the same-stream case of (2)) 0/800. Cost: one 64-byte ring slot per
event record, on gfx7 and gfx8 only, and no change to any copy path's engine, so
D2H and H2D keep the copy engine. Measured bandwidth is unchanged inside noise
(D2H pinned 2.07 to 2.03 GiB/s, H2D pinned 4.83 to 4.76 GiB/s).

WHAT
----
`rocclr/utils/flags.hpp`: new `CLR_GFX8_TC_INVALIDATE` (default on), so the
behavior can be measured both ways without a rebuild.

`rocclr/device/rocm/rocvirtual.{hpp,cpp}`: `VirtualGPU::insertTcFence()` reserves
one 64-byte AQL slot and fills it with ACQUIRE_MEM (full address range,
TC_ACTION_ENA|TC_WB_ACTION_ENA), a NOP, and the trailing RELEASE_MEM whose
EVENT_INDEX(AQL) retires the slot. That is the PM4-inside-an-AQL-slot protocol
that ROCr's gfx8 `ExecutePM4()` already uses, with the payload inlined instead of
fetched through an indirect buffer. dword 0 is stored last with release semantics,
so the CP never sees a half-built packet, and the packet that follows rings the
doorbell at a higher ring index, which covers this slot too. The slot publishes no
completion signal of its own. Three call sites:

  * `dispatchGenericAqlPacket()`, for the kernel-dispatch packet types only.
  * `releaseGpuMemoryFence()`, between its barrier and a second barrier, which
    covers every non-shader reader of GPU memory, because both
    `DmaBlitManager::readBuffer` and `writeBuffer` fence there before SDMA.
  * `submitMarker()`, when the marker is an event record
    (`vcmd.coalesceEvent() != 0`), which is the point where this queue's results
    become readable by whoever waits on that event. It is the raw slot only: no
    barrier, no completion signal, so it takes no part in the marker's own signal
    bookkeeping.

CAVEAT FOR THE NEXT PERSON: the HIP graph replay path
(`VirtualGPU::dispatchAqlPacketBatchFlat`) copies captured packets into the ring in
bulk and is not covered, so those dispatches carry no ACQUIRE_MEM. A graph that
reads a VA recycled inside its own replay is exposed in principle. Measured: a
torch `CUDAGraph` capture of exactly the poisoned sequence (bmm x2 + bmm(a,b) +
`z.copy_(y)`, 40 replays, destination read after each) came out 0/40 poisoned with
the knob off as well as on, so the hole is unproven rather than known broken.
Closing it needs the slot inserted per dispatch inside the batch assembly, which
touches signal attachment and metadata indexing, and it is deliberately left undone
here rather than half done.

COST: one extra 64-byte ring slot plus a full-range TC invalidate per dispatch, on
gfx7 and gfx8 only. That is half the ring's usable depth, which matters on this
part, because the queue cap is 64 packets (see
graph-replay-batch-chunk-deadlock.patch). The slot-stall wait in
`WaitForQueueSlot()` keeps it correct when the ring fills. (2) adds
barrier, slot, and barrier per pre-copy fence. A fence happens per host-visible
transfer and per device sync, not per kernel. (3) adds one slot per event record
and nothing at all to a copy path's choice of engine, so D2H and H2D keep SDMA. Measured on the card: D2H +2.7%,
H2D +1.5%, one-element sync 3.6 to 4.2 us, and compute unchanged (fp16 2048^3 GEMM
+0.04%, conv and GPU-to-GPU clone unchanged).

Applies on top of the chain up to fmm-keep-userptr-map. It touches the same file as
d2h-staged-copy, d2h-null-dsthost, and pinned-release-system-scope, but different
functions. Apply with
patches/rocm-systems/gfx803-tc-invalidate-acquire-mem.sh.

MEASURED ON REAL HARDWARE (gfx803 RX 470, 2026-09-05)
-----------------------------------------------------
Reader direction, one binary with the knob toggled: the bmm and copy probe 15/40
to 0/40 (also 25/40 to 0/40 and 22/40 to 0/40). A 527-check soak across bmm,
conv+silu+groupnorm, linear+layernorm+softmax, `scaled_dot_product_attention`,
reductions, and gather/scatter went from 91 failures to 0, and repeated clean over
five seeds (2635 checks). A 60-step Adam training loop went from
not-reproducible-with-itself (53/60 gradient frozen-copy mismatches, 47/60 steps
with the wrong loss against CPU) to bit-identical with the CPU loss trajectory.
`ghost2.py` gives 0/40. External-reader direction: the conv probe went 90/240 to
0/240.
Cross-queue direction, one binary with `CLR_GFX8_TC_RECORD_FENCE` toggled:
`ms3.py` 0 anomalies in 2000 checks on, 21 in 960 off; `multistream2.py` 0/1600;
`ms4.py` (pinned destination, and two producers fanning into one consumer) 0/2400.
Same binary, other probes: `xprobe2.py` 0/40, `pinnedhost.py` 0/60, `conbrace3.py`
0/800. Copy bandwidth unchanged inside noise (D2H pinned 2.07 to 2.03 GiB/s, H2D
pinned 4.83 to 4.76 GiB/s), because no copy is moved off the copy engine.
Torch op suite on the box stack: 202/202 PASS, 0 BAD, 0 NONFINITE, both with a
hand-built library and with `libamdhip64` taken from the image this chain produces.
Inside the image the suite reports 118 PASS, 0 BAD, 0 NONFINITE and 84 ERRORs that
are all `/ INDUCTOR`: the image ships no Triton, so inductor cannot compile, which
is a content question about the image and not a wrong answer. sd1.5
txt2img x6 with no GPU resets, and dmesg clean of resets and VM faults
throughout. `tools/tc-staleness/README.md` holds the harnesses and the expected
numbers.

Not measured, and open: HIP graph replay (see the caveat).

